c: Complete flexible array member initializers of compound literals

A static (C23 storage-class specifier) or file-scope compound literal of
a struct type whose flexible array member is initialized, e.g.

  int g (void)
  { return __builtin_strlen ((static struct { char i, d[]; }) { 0, 0 }.d); }

was handled by build_compound_literal without the two steps that
finish_decl performs for an ordinary variable with such an initializer:
adding the size of the flexible array member's initializer to the
DECL_SIZE of the object (add_flexible_array_elts_to_size), and giving
the initializer's array type a domain (complete_flexible_array_elts).

As a result the object was emitted with a DECL_SIZE that does not cover
the initializer, so varasm produced a negative amount of trailing
padding ('.space 18446744073709551609' / '.zero -7'), which the
assembler rejects; and component_ref_size, when asked for the size of
the member by the waccess pass (at -O0) or the strlen pass (at -O2),
found an initializer whose type has no TYPE_SIZE_UNIT and passed the
null tree to tree_to_poly_int64, crashing with a segfault.

Do both steps in build_compound_literal, and make component_ref_size
return "unknown" instead of crashing if it ever sees such an initializer
from another source.

---
--- a/gcc/c/c-decl.cc
+++ b/gcc/c/c-decl.cc
@@ -6520,6 +6520,17 @@
       DECL_USER_ALIGN (decl) = 1;
     }
   store_init_value (loc, decl, init, NULL_TREE);
+
+  /* As in finish_decl, if the initializer initializes a flexible array
+     member, add the size of its initializer to the size of the object
+     and give the initializer's array type a domain.  Otherwise the
+     object would be emitted with too small a size, and the middle end
+     (e.g. component_ref_size) would see an initializer whose type has
+     no size.  */
+  if (init && TREE_CODE (init) == CONSTRUCTOR)
+    add_flexible_array_elts_to_size (decl, init);
+  complete_flexible_array_elts (DECL_INITIAL (decl));
+
   if (current_scope != file_scope
       && TREE_STATIC (decl)
       && !TREE_READONLY (decl)
--- a/gcc/tree.cc
+++ b/gcc/tree.cc
@@ -13613,6 +13613,11 @@
